+---
+id: task-003-fix-welcome-code-reuse-after-cancel-free-shipping-at-50-euro
+type: task
+title: "Fix welcome code reuse after cancel; free shipping at 50 euro"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Request
+
+1. Cancelling an order did not give its single-use coupon back: `OrderService.cancel` passed the
+   customer name to `Coupons.giveBack` instead of the order id, so the coupon stayed used. Fixed in
+   `src/orders.ts`; test added in `test/orders.test.ts`.
+2. Free shipping at exactly €50.00: the rule (`goods >= 5000`, after every discount) is already correct
+   in `src/shipping.ts` since task-001; I could not reproduce €4.90 on €50.00 goods. A regression test
+   covers exactly €50.00 and a coupon case. If the complaint persists, it probably predates that fix
+   or the goods were really below €50.00 after discounts.
+
diff --git a/src/orders.ts b/src/orders.ts
index 0c0b1ae889c9df13dffa9de7ed1924a4e8b09afd..e4c175527cc500b240776198678b04a3145894ea 100644
--- a/src/orders.ts
+++ b/src/orders.ts
@@ -153,7 +153,7 @@ export class OrderService {
       throw new OrderStateError(`order ${id} can no longer be cancelled`);
     }
     for (const line of order.lines) this.#inventory.release(line.sku, line.quantity, id);
-    if (order.coupon !== undefined) this.#coupons.giveBack(order.coupon, order.customer);
+    if (order.coupon !== undefined) this.#coupons.giveBack(order.coupon, id);
     const cancelled: Order = { ...order, status: 'cancelled', cancelledOn: date };
     this.#orders.set(id, cancelled);
     return cancelled;
diff --git a/test/orders.test.ts b/test/orders.test.ts
index d07f06bd09b3489b674e3119a14ea7d2ced148a7..a12f2adc26c5548c98a0736fc4f846288f741fe8 100644
--- a/test/orders.test.ts
